window.docContents[226].resolve({"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws--Symmetric-First-Order-Systems--Scope-of-the-energy-method":{"contents":"Mollifying the spatial operator first gives a bounded-operator evolution\nproblem. For coefficients continuous in time in spatial  norm,\nthe construction works for each  with  initial data\nand  forcing. Symmetric spatial  coefficients\nwith uniformly bounded derivative norms give an energy bound independent\nof .\n\nWith  initial data and  forcing, we can also pass to the\noriginal system. On a finite interval , with , assume the\nsymmetric matrix entries belong to spatial . Their values\nmust be continuous in time in spatial  norm on that interval,\nand their spatial derivative norms must be uniformly bounded there.\nUnder these hypotheses the construction gives an \nsolution of the original principal system,\nwith  time derivative and a continuous  representative\nattaining the initial data.\n\nThe proof bounds the mollified trajectories in , passes to a joint\nweak Bochner limit of states and time derivatives, and identifies the\nunmollified coefficient terms. Weak closedness preserves the time-dependent\nenergy bound. The integrated initial identity supplies the continuous\n trace. The principal system has no zeroth-order coefficient.\n\nThe same coefficient assumptions also allow merely  initial data and\nprescribed forcing  square-integrable on time-space. Almost every\nspatial slice of  defines an  class; constructing these classes\nas a Bochner forcing lets the Cauchy argument apply.\nThe resulting continuous  curve attains the initial datum. Its energy\nbound uses the sum of the product integrals of , without a factor\nfor the number of components.\n\nThe weak equation uses this same . Every joint product- state\nsatisfying that equation with the same initial boundary datum agrees with\nthe constructed state almost everywhere for the product measure.\nReconstructing a competitor's Bochner sections reduces this to uniqueness\nof the Bochner class, without assuming time regularity of the competitor.\nThe initial value is attained by the continuous  curve and enters the\nweak equation through its initial boundary term.\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Symmetric Systems and Conservation Laws\u0009Symmetric First-Order Systems","header":"7.5.1.1. Scope of the energy method","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws--Symmetric-First-Order-Systems--Scope-of-the-energy-method"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--The-Evolution-Problem-at-Energy-Regularity":{"contents":"For a parabolic equation, the spatial energy often places the solution in a\nspace , while the time derivative is meaningful only in the dual\n. The initial condition, however, belongs naturally to an intermediate\nHilbert space . A Gelfand triple\n\n\n\nplaces the weak equation in\n for almost every time, and the initial value is interpreted through a\ncontinuous -valued representative.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Weak Evolution\u0009Weak Evolution and Galerkin Approximation","header":"6.3.1.1. The Evolution Problem at Energy Regularity","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--The-Evolution-Problem-at-Energy-Regularity"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Time-Regularity-for-Weak-Evolution":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Weak Evolution\u0009Weak Evolution and Galerkin Approximation","header":"6.3.1.6. Time Regularity for Weak Evolution","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Time-Regularity-for-Weak-Evolution"}});